  ## Tests and verification

  Passed:

  - go test -count=1 ./...
  - go test -cover -count=1 ./... plus the remaining core packages explicitly: all passed.
  - go vet ./...
  - go build -trimpath -o /dev/null ./cmd/gator
  - go run ./cmd/gator version
  - go run ./cmd/gator help

  Coverage is meaningful but uneven: agent loop 86.0%, app server 80.1%, run executor 74.3%, terminal 71.9%, tools 69.9%, sandbox 63.7%, TUI 61.8%; CLI entrypoint is only
  45.6% and eval is 39.6%. There are two fuzz targets, both in the app-server HTTP/parser area, and no benchmarks.

  Incomplete or missing verification:

  - No full race-detector run.
  - No fuzzing campaign.
  - No live-provider tests; provider tests use local HTTP mocks.
  - No broad Linux sandbox-escape regression test—the explicit “cannot write outside worktree” test is macOS-only.
  - No real browser, LSP, MCP, OAuth, or multi-agent dogfood evidence against external systems.
  - No real-model evaluation or comparative benchmark.

  ## Recommendation

  Do not add more features yet. First fix formatting, add a license/security/release provenance baseline, and create a public evaluation and dogfood program with real
  repositories, multiple languages, strict sandboxing, live providers, failure cases, cost/latency, and reproducible results.

  Until that exists, market Gator as an experimental security-first harness, not as a replacement for established coding-agent products.
